# kernel_exports.s -- GENERATED from the core kernel's build listing, for every
# consumer that is assembled separately and therefore cannot see the core's
# symbols.  The four modules were always ignored; the far bank's copy was
# tracked, alone among the five, and that was the whole of apple/fgx#79: it
# holds ABSOLUTE addresses, so every commit that changes the core kernel's size
# rewrote it, and two branches that both touched the kernel conflicted on it
# every time -- with neither side correct for the merged result.
#
# It is safe to ignore because there is NO CYCLE: it is generated FROM the core
# listing and consumed BY the far bank afterwards, and the build already states
# that ordering (see gen.py's note on CI job 479, where the missing dependency
# made merlin32 run before the file was written).  A clean checkout without it
# is the expected case, not a broken one.
#
# The three PATCH TABLES are different and stay tracked: each is an input to
# the very assembly whose listing generates it, so there is no clean-checkout
# state for them.  They are safe tracked because a stale copy fails the build
# LOUDLY -- all three are --check'd in the kchecks rule, and the two bank-patch
# tables carry symbolic entries the assembler refuses when a site disappears.
# kernel_build_id.s -- the core build identity, GENERATED beside them and
# ignored for the same reason.  It is an INPUT to the kernel's own assembly,
# which would normally put it in the "stays tracked" group below with the patch
# tables -- except that it derives from the module MANIFESTS in
# gen_kernel_exports.py and NOT from any listing, so there is no cycle and a
# clean checkout without it is the expected case.  Tracking it would rewrite a
# tracked file on every build for no gain.  A stale copy cannot ship quietly:
# check_build_id.py compares it against the module side and fails the build.
src/kernel/kernel_build_id.s
src/modules/serial/kernel_exports.s
src/modules/prodos/kernel_exports.s
src/modules/bpf/kernel_exports.s
